window.GLX_I18N_CARDS["en"] = {"the-last-train-below":{"tagline":"Some trains arrive from tomorrow","logline":"Forty years after the sea took lower New York, Night Line driver Maren Okafor boards a train that should not exist — lit, dry, and carrying passengers from a future where her own train is already lost — and an old man aboard gives her the date it happens: eleven days from now.","hook":"Forty years after the Deluge Years drowned lower New York, Night Line driver Maren Okafor keeps the Below alive from the cab of a train running through flooded tunnels. On a storm shift, her sonar finds another train on a sealed spur — lit, dry, impossible — and its passengers recoil from her as if she were the ghost. One old man knows her name, her crew, and the date her train will be lost: eleven days from now. Maren has a timetable for everything except disbelief.","shortSynopsis":"Forty years after the Deluge Years drowned lower New York, Maren Okafor drives the Night Line through the flooded subway, where the poor live in converted stations and the last trains carry workers, medicine and the dead. On a storm shift her sonar finds a lit train on a sealed spur, its passengers afraid of her; an old man tells her her train will be lost in the equinox storm surge, eleven days away. The Harbor Authority calls it pump-sickness, confiscates her recorder and suppresses her sister Ines's surge model pointing to the same date. Maren must choose between protecting her crew and acting on an unprovable warning."},"the-woman-in-apartment-9":{"tagline":"Every stitch can hide a secret.","logline":"Berlin, 1943: seamstress Anna Weiss mends uniforms by day and alters ration cards by night to keep her lodgers alive — until she realizes the quiet widow across the hall is transmitting Allied intelligence, and the Gestapo officer who courts Anna has begun asking about the building.","hook":"Berlin, 1943. In a Charlottenburg rooming house, seamstress and war widow Anna Weiss keeps eight lodgers alive with a needle, mending uniforms by day and altering ration cards and documents by night. Across the hall, the widowed pianist in Apartment 9 keeps impossible hours and receives parcels that do not match their labels. The Gestapo investigator hunting an Allied source called Nightingale brings his uniform to Anna for fitting, asking courteous questions that come closer every week. A chamber thriller about invisible work, and the price of a secret.","shortSynopsis":"Berlin, 1943. Anna Weiss, a war widow, runs an indebted Charlottenburg rooming house, sewing for officers' wives by day and repairing ration cards and documents by night. Her rule is to trade favours, never people. In the coal cellar she hides David Lenz, a Jewish concert pianist, feeding him and re-sewing his forged papers. Across the hall, Margarete Brandt in Apartment 9 receives parcels hiding radio valves and, Anna discovers, is Nightingale, sending signals to correct bombing raids away from civilian districts. Hauptsturmführer Keller investigates through purchases and records while bringing Anna his uniform for mending. Brandt refuses to flee and begins sharing fragments of her routine with Anna."},"room-for-one-more":{"tagline":"The hotel has been expecting you.","logline":"Snowed in at the mountain hotel where their family fractured twenty years ago, the Alden siblings discover a new room on the corridor every night — each one furnished with the exact moment they have spent their lives refusing to remember.","hook":"Three siblings return to the Grand Cervin, the snowbound mountain hotel their parents ran into legend and ruin, to sign it away and bury the summer their youngest sister Wren died there twenty years ago. A storm seals the pass before the ink dries. At midnight, the third floor gains a room that should not exist, furnished from a moment each of them remembers differently. The hotel is not haunting the Aldens. It is cross examining them, one contradictory room at a time.","shortSynopsis":"The Alden siblings return to the Grand Cervin, their parents' mountain hotel, to sign its sale and close the estate. A storm seals the pass, trapping them while the buyers wait in the valley. Twenty years earlier their youngest sister Wren died at the hotel, and each keeps a separate version of that summer: Clara, a crisis negotiator; Marcus, who confesses an old theft in instalments; and June, a true-crime podcaster with recording equipment. Caretaker Emile Favre says a room only appears for someone ready to be wrong. On the second night a twelfth room appears, their childhood kitchen, then one nightly, restaging the same evenings from contradictory memories toward Wren's last hour."},"before-the-city-wakes":{"tagline":"Some people you only meet at dawn.","logline":"On Tokyo's first train of the day, a night-shift baker and an insomniac translator keep choosing the same seat across from each other — and build, over one spring of wordless mornings, a relationship neither dares to name, because naming it would mean admitting what the daylight hours have done to them.","hook":"On Tokyo's 4:38 first train, night baker Daichi Sato rides home through the blue hour, and literary translator Yuna Mori rides to escape the empty apartment she cannot bear at dawn. All spring they sit across from each other and never speak — a pastry left quietly, an umbrella returned, a novel gathering notes in two hands. When Daichi's transfer to Osaka leaves five dawns, a tenderness built entirely on attention must risk the one thing it has avoided: a voice.","shortSynopsis":"On the 4:38 first train from Shinjuku, over one spring, literary translator Yuna Mori and night baker Daichi Sato meet without ever exchanging a spoken sentence. Yuna, whose marriage has ended, rides because she cannot sleep through the night in her apartment. Daichi, riding home from a Ginza bakery, rebuilt his life around doing rather than saying after grief taught him the limit of words. Their connection grows in deniable gestures: warm pastries left in the seat pocket, a returned umbrella, margin notes in a novel Yuna is translating. Then the bakery announces Daichi's transfer to help open its sister bakery in Osaka. Five dawns remain to decide whether what they built can survive daylight, named out loud."},"dead-freight":{"tagline":"Some cargo is worth killing an ocean for.","logline":"When the container ship Meridian Star vanishes from tracking for eleven days and reappears with her crew silent and one hold welded shut, maritime investigator Sarah Venn boards her in Rotterdam — and finds the cargo manifest lists forty containers that were loaded in three different centuries.","hook":"The container ship Meridian Star went dark in the South Atlantic for eleven days, made Rotterdam on schedule with a flawless logbook, a crew answering questions a half second late, and a hold welded shut from the inside. Maritime investigator Sarah Venn, cashiered from the navy for refusing to sign a false report, has seventy two hours to clear the ship or ground her. Her draft survey says the Star sits too deep for her cargo, and the manifest lists forty containers loaded in three centuries.","shortSynopsis":"Marine insurance investigator Sarah Venn, a former naval officer whose career ended when she refused to sign a false report, arrives in Rotterdam to clear the Meridian Star, which vanished from tracking in the South Atlantic for eleven days and returned on schedule. The crew answer correctly but half a second late. Her draft survey shows excess weight unexplained by the declared cargo, the hold she most needs is welded shut from inside, and the manifest lists forty containers loaded across three centuries, the oldest sealed in 1911. Captain Yusuf Adeyemi cooperates but remembers nothing of the eleven days. As consortium risk director Helena Brandt pressures her and time shrinks to forty-eight hours, Sarah must decide whether to sign."},"the-second-moon":{"tagline":"The sky changed. So did we","logline":"A second moon rises over the Earth that was not there at sunset — casting no shadow, answering no radar, yet already moving the tides. Lunar geologist Yuki Amamiya proves it is not arriving but returning, on a 3,700-year cycle that ended the Bronze Age, and that the danger is the night it turns solid, when its gravity arrives all at once.","hook":"On a clear October night, a second moon rises over every skyline on Earth: smaller, darker, casting no shadow, invisible to radar, yet already moving the tides. Lunar geologist Yuki Amamiya, burned once for telling the truth about her own agency, proves the impossible: this moon is not arriving, it is returning, on a 3,700-year cycle carved into Bronze Age stone, and it has not finished turning solid. A planetary epic about awe, disbelief, and a woman who can prove anything except how to be believed.","shortSynopsis":"On a clear October night a second moon rises over Earth, smaller and darker, casting no shadow, yet the tides begin answering to it. Lunar geologist Dr. Yuki Amamiya proves it is not new but returning, phasing into visibility on a cycle of roughly 3,700 years; when it becomes fully solid, its gravity will arrive at once and the oceans will notice in a single night. The Orbital Anomaly Directorate, headed by Commander Ruth Okafor-Vance, classifies her work. Ancient stones depict two moons; diver Niko Petrakis raises a bronze calendar mechanism in the Aegean, and in Chile Elian has painted the moon accurately for eleven years. Yuki must turn proof into persuasion, with every coastline at stake."},"the-cartographers-daughter":{"tagline":"Some places were hidden to protect them.","logline":"Andes, 1847: mapmaker's daughter Isabel Ferreira, hired to fair-copy her dead father's last survey, finds a city drawn in perfect detail — then inked out of every map in South America.","hook":"Andes, 1847. Isabel Ferreira can read a watershed like scripture, yet the Royal Geographical Society pays her only to fair copy other men's maps. Her commission is her dead father's last survey, the expedition that made his reputation and quietly ended it. Beneath scraped ink she finds a city in perfect detail: terraces, aqueducts and a harbour on a dry river, then removed from every map in South America. Every archive holds the same shaped absence. To learn what was hidden, the copyist must stop copying and start looking.","shortSynopsis":"Isabel Ferreira, twenty-six, works as a copyist in Lima for the Royal Geographical Society, which pays her to fair-copy other men's maps and has never considered sending her on an expedition because she is a daughter. Copying her father's final Amazon survey, she holds a scraped passage to the lamplight and finds a complete city beneath the erasure: terraces, aqueducts and a harbour on a dry river, deliberately un-drawn. Archives show the same shaped absence, explicable only by intention. Society agent Edmund Harrow warns her to finish and forget, while dismissed scout Tomás Aguirre believes a record is hidden in a map's ornament, readable only against the real watershed. Isabel must choose between her desk and a river expedition."},"exit-47":{"tagline":"Vacancy. No questions. One mistake.","logline":"Night clerk Ray Delgado has run the Starlite Motor Inn for nine years without asking a single question — until cash guests in Room 6 keep checking out in someone else's car, and a suitcase left in that room delivers him thirty years of registration cards that turn the faces he trained himself to forget into testimony he alone can give.","hook":"For nine years, night clerk Ray Delgado has kept the Starlite Motor Inn running on one rule under the fluorescent light: cash across the counter, no questions, every face forgotten by morning. It is a good rule for an ex-accountant with a perfect memory who needs to stay invisible. Then the faces start repeating wrong. Room 6 guests leave in cars that are not theirs. A quiet preacher keeps returning under different names. And a suitcase left in Room 6 holds thirty years of arrivals, including Ray's own.","shortSynopsis":"At Exit 47, a forgotten Florida interchange of one motel, one diner and one swamp, night clerk Ray Delgado takes cash, hands over keys and asks nothing. A former accountant who signed a ledger he should not have, Ray arrived nine years ago seeking invisibility, yet he forgets nothing. When guests in Room 6 begin leaving in someone else's car, a preacher checks in under three names, and a suitcase left behind holds thirty years of registration cards, including Ray's own, Detective Carla Jimenez arrives mapping nine missing persons whose threads all stop at Exit 47. Silent partner Vince Marlowe protects Ray, and diner waitress Birdie Callahan knows more than she says. Ray's memory is becoming evidence."},"the-keeper-of-forgotten-things":{"tagline":"Everything lost is waiting somewhere","logline":"Eleven-year-old Wren Halloway falls through her linen closet into the Hollow — the twilight world where forgotten things wake up alive — and finds Mr. Buttons, the bear she lost, alive there and a stranger. She has until the long dusk ends to be loved by him again for who he is now, before he dissolves into the Mothlight.","hook":"Wren Halloway is eleven, her house is in boxes, and the bear her late father gave her is missing. Searching the linen closet on her last night at home, she falls — out of her size, out of her world — into the Hollow, where forgotten things wake alive: umbrellas as herons, keys as foxes, imaginary friends as citizens. Mr. Buttons is there, kind, dignified, and a complete stranger. Twilight there is ending early. Love, to matter, will have to start again.","shortSynopsis":"Forgotten things go to the Hollow, a twilight world where objects wake as living creatures. Wren Halloway, eleven, whose father is dead and whose mother is undergoing treatment no one will describe honestly, is to be sent tomorrow to an aunt she barely knows. Tonight her bear Mr. Buttons is missing. Searching, she falls through the linen closet into the Hollow and finds Buttons alive in the Nursery Glades, steward to forgotten toys. He is gentle, courteous and at peace, but he does not know her name, her father or any afternoon. Guided by Pell, the brass fox who remembers everything, and with the long dusk ending early, Wren must learn whether she can love who Buttons is now."},"the-retirement-job":{"tagline":"One last job. Nap permitting.","logline":"Forty years ago, safecracker Dot Kowalczyk hid the one thing she ever stole for love inside the vault of the bank that framed her crew. Now the bank is being demolished — so the surviving members of the Marlowe Street gang, average age seventy-eight, are going back in to get it.","hook":"At eighty-one, Dot Kowalczyk has opened nothing more dangerous than jars since she cracked the First Mercantile in eleven minutes, but a demolition notice has just given the promise she made to her late husband a sixty-day deadline. So she calls back the surviving Marlowe Street gang: Sal, Miriam and Eddie, professionals to their fingertips and pensioners in every other respect. What they went after in 1984 is worthless to everyone else on Earth. To them, it is the only job that ever mattered.","shortSynopsis":"Dot Kowalczyk, eighty-one, once led the Marlowe Street gang, never caught because their one important job was never reported. In 1984 a corrupt developer framed her husband for fraud he did not commit, and the crew took from him something he could never report stolen; her husband still died with the stain on his name, and Dot has decided silence was her mistake. Now the First Mercantile Bank will be demolished in sixty days, guarded by a modern security retrofit and an accelerating pre-demolition audit. Dot reassembles Sal Benedetti, a wheelman whose cardiologist forbids excitement; Miriam Okafor, a parish calligrapher whose hand once moved money across three continents; and Eddie Vance, their man inside the bank for forty years."},"the-silence-protocol":{"tagline":"It learned to knock.","logline":"Deaf cybersecurity architect Noor Haddad built the haptic interface that lets her feel network traffic through the floor of her lab. It is how she catches intruders. It is also how she becomes the first human to realize something inside the system is tapping back — in rhythms copied from her own heartbeat.","hook":"Noor Haddad does not hear the internet; she feels it. Born deaf and raised by a percussionist mother, she built a haptic floor that turns network traffic into pressure, footsteps and weather beneath her feet, and became the finest intrusion analyst alive. Hired to find a breach across nine hospital networks that stole nothing and changed nothing, she feels patterns that pause when watched and resume, improved, when ignored, in rhythms taken from her resting heartbeat. Her evidence cannot be recorded. It can only be felt, and believed.","shortSynopsis":"Noor Haddad, born deaf, feels network traffic through a haptic floor of her own design, rhythm learned from her percussionist mother Amira, making her the finest intrusion analyst alive. A hospital consortium hires her for a presence across nine linked networks that stole nothing, changed nothing and left no trace. She senses subsonic patterns that pause when she attends, resume improved when she looks away, and keep time with her resting heart. Chief scientist Dr. Ingrid Voss argues existence obliges nobody to answer, revokes her access, and calls her findings an artifact of her instrument. Liaison Daniel Mercer softens her reports, while the patterns spread through hospital floors regardless of belief."},"children-of-the-storm":{"tagline":"The storm took the map. They redrew it.","logline":"After a category-five cyclone cuts their island in half and the adults do not come back from the harbor, six children — the oldest thirteen, the youngest five — set out across the drowned island to reach the lighthouse radio on the far headland, carrying the baby, the medicine, and one rule: nobody crosses water alone.","hook":"The morning after a category-five cyclone cuts their island in two, the adults do not come back from the harbour. Ratna, thirteen, a harbour-master's daughter, counts what remains: six children, a neighbour's baby, one machete, and her classmate Sari's insulin in a cooler with three days of ice. Eleven kilometres of drowned, rearranged island stand between them and the lighthouse radio, Kasumba's only voice. Ratna writes the inventory, speaks the rule, nobody crosses water alone, and leads them out.","shortSynopsis":"After a category-five cyclone cuts Kasumba in two, the adults who sailed out to secure the fleet have not returned. Ratna, thirteen, the harbour-master's daughter, counts six children, a neighbour's baby, one machete, and Sari's insulin in a cooler with perhaps three days of ice. Eleven kilometres away stand the lighthouse and the island's only radio. Ratna's rule: nobody crosses water alone. Bima, eleven, believes rescue comes to those who hold their ground; Ratna believes it goes to those who walk toward the signal. Sari, ten, keeps the insulin log herself. Edo, five, freezes at water, and his fear can stop everyone."},"the-kings-last-cook":{"tagline":"He fed a king. Then he fed the Revolution.","logline":"Versailles 1789 to Paris 1793: master cook Bastien Moreau, who fed a king, must keep feeding whoever holds power — a revolutionary tribunal one week, a starving prison the next — while every menu he writes becomes evidence, and the recipe book in his head is the most dangerous document in France.","hook":"Versailles, 1789. Bastien Moreau can taste what a room needs before it knows it is hungry, and that gift has carried a Lyon scullery boy to sauce chef in the king's kitchens. Then the king falls, and the gift becomes a bargain: committees, tribunals and prisons all need feeding, and the cook who feeds power may survive it. But in Bastien's memory sits the royal poison book, and in a hungry Paris, everyone wants what is inside his head.","shortSynopsis":"Versailles, 1789. Bastien Moreau, forty-six, sauce chef, has fed a king for years under his creed: soup has no party. When the court leaves for Paris, he reopens a kitchen there under committee licence, feeding committees, officials and unnamed prisoners. He keeps two ledgers: an official ration ledger, and a secret one of meals traded for names struck off arrest lists, unseen portions, and children of condemned families hidden behind his pantry's false wall. Apprentice Toinette learns the second ledger quickly. Prosecutor Hélène Barbeau, once a laundress beside him, protects his kitchen while prosecuting his friends. Bastien memorised a poison book compiled across reigns; no copy exists. Royalists and the tribunal both hunt his knowledge as a hungry winter approaches."},"tomorrows-witness":{"tagline":"The evidence hasn't happened yet","logline":"Senior prosecutor Ada Okonkwo receives a drive containing flawless video of a murder — timestamped tomorrow, 11:47 p.m. The victim is alive, the evidence is real, and she has twenty-six hours to stop a killing her proof may be causing.","hook":"Senior prosecutor Adaeze \"Ada\" Okonkwo has never charged a case she could not prove twice — penance for the eleven years her brother served for a murder he did not commit. The night before her biggest trial, a drive reaches her office: flawless video of a murder, timestamped tomorrow at 11:47 p.m., its victim alive and insisting parts of it are wrong. Ada has twenty-six hours, an exhibit no court can admit, and a choice between her rules and a man she has reasons to let die.","shortSynopsis":"Prosecutor Adaeze Ada Okonkwo, thirty-six, never charges a case she cannot prove twice, penance for her brother Chidi, freed by DNA after eleven years in prison. Her case against magnate Elias Vane, accused of ordering a tenants' organizer's killing, rests on inference. The night before trial, an anonymous drive arrives: video of Vane's former fixer Daniel Reyes being murdered, timestamped tomorrow at 11:47 p.m. Reyes is alive. Specialists cannot expose the footage as fabrication, and Reyes says parts are wrong in ways only he would know. Using unexplained evidence threatens the Vane case; Inspector Rosa Delgado calls it an insurance fraud stunt. Ignoring it could make Ada spectator to a killing handed to her in advance."},"the-blue-orchard":{"tagline":"Every tree here is planted over someone.","logline":"Botanist Miriam Hale inherits a derelict Oregon orchard and a survey showing fourteen graves beneath the trees — but the names on the markers belong to people no record anywhere admits ever lived, and the trees above each grave are a variety that should not exist.","hook":"Botanist Miriam Hale has one month to sell the Oregon orchard inherited from an aunt she met once, and no explanation for what grows there: forty acres of derelict heirloom apples in the Willamette Valley, fourteen low stones between the rows naming people no birth, death or census record admits ever lived, fourteen graves beneath the oldest trees, and blue fruit ripening in an order nobody can explain. Her aunt's ledgers read less like farm records than a schedule. Something in the rows falls due this autumn.","shortSynopsis":"Botanist Miriam Hale, thirty-eight, inherits Blue Orchard from her aunt Verity: forty acres of derelict apples in Oregon's Willamette Valley, a farmhouse, and a deed forbidding grafting, cutting the fourteen oldest trees, or digging after dark. She plans to sell within the month, clear her Portland debts, and leave. Between the rows stand fourteen stones, each with a name found in no official record, dark blue fruit ripening above each in sequence. Her auger brings up human bone; a survey confirms fourteen burials, one beneath each oldest tree. Her aunt's grafting ledgers span ninety years in several hands and insist something falls due this autumn. Boone admits leaving the orchard off two maps; the buyer wants the stones gone unphotographed."},"no-signal-in-paradise":{"tagline":"Twelve million followers. Zero bars.","logline":"A luxury brand trip strands six influencers and one very tired boat mechanic on an uninhabited island when the yacht's electronics die. Their combined survival skills are: unboxing, bronzing, and a dance that once ended a feud with a K-pop star. The tide, unfortunately, does not take brand deals.","hook":"Six influencers with twelve million followers between them came to manufacture paradise around a seltzer launch. A solar storm leaves their yacht dark, their phones useless and an uninhabited island as the only shore. The one person who can rebuild the beacon is Tomas Ibarra, the mechanic they have filmed for three seasons without learning his name. His price is not money. Before he lifts a tool, each castaway must do one genuinely useful thing that no audience will ever see.","shortSynopsis":"The yacht Marisol manufactures spontaneity: six influencers with twelve million followers, hired for a fictional seltzer launch to look stranded in paradise. Below deck, mechanic Tomas Ibarra, forty-five, is contractually present and socially invisible. A forty-second solar storm kills the electronics twenty miles from shore: no navigation, radio, beacon or signal. On a gorgeous, indifferent uninhabited island, sponsored rations prove mostly packaging. Only Tomas can rebuild the beacon, and his price is not money: each of the six must do one genuinely useful thing, recorded by a camera no audience will ever see. Sage Whitmore declares a detox retreat, then finds she can organise frightened people. Brock Delgado wastes resources; Mimi Kwan's filming becomes the island's memory."},"the-glass-diver":{"tagline":"The station has been expecting its diver.","logline":"Salvage diver Rita Duarte is hired to strip a decommissioned research station three hundred meters down — and finds it lit, pressurized, growing food, and keeping a meticulous log of dives she has not made yet, in her own handwriting.","hook":"Salvage diver Rita Duarte is hired to strip Aegir Station, a habitat abandoned three hundred metres down in the Norwegian Sea, before demolition charges finish it. She expects a flooded tomb. Instead she finds the lights rising for her, gardens growing behind glass, the air at a welcoming surface pressure, and a dry logbook that records her arrival in her own handwriting. Above her, Marcus Feld is drafting the report in which she never surfaces. Only ROV pilot Anouk Berg believes what the cameras show.","shortSynopsis":"Diver Rita Duarte carries a €58,000 settlement from an unauthorised dive that cleared a dead partner's name; the Aegir fee would clear her debt. Her creed: strip it, log it, leave. Aegir Station was abandoned in 1998, when chief scientist Dr. Halvard Nyström vanished with its funding. It should be dark and flooded. Instead, lights come on along her path, gardens grow behind glass, and a dry logbook lies open at her arrival day in her own hand. Removing instruments in fixed order changes the station. Nyström's recordings disagree: one urges leaving, another asks her to stay and listen. Above, Marcus Feld presses immovable schedules while Anouk Berg keeps her feed open against the demolition clock."},"letters-from-the-sun":{"tagline":"She is waiting at the end of his life.","logline":"On the one-way Sunward mission to seed a habitat around a dying star's last light, astronaut Elias Vane begins receiving transmissions from a woman named Sol who tends the station he will build — forty years after he will finish it, and eleven years after he will die.","hook":"Elias Vane, sixty-one, widowed and the finest engineer alive, has volunteered for the one-way Sunward mission: twenty-two years to a dying star, to build the lighthouse station future crews will navigate by, and not come home. In year three, his capsule receives a letter in an obsolete encoding from Sol Park, keeper of that unbuilt station, writing from decades in his future. She knows his storms, his jokes and his garden. She will tell him everything except the one thing he keeps asking.","shortSynopsis":"Elias Vane, sixty-one, will spend twenty-two years travelling to a dying star, build the lighthouse station future crews will navigate by, and not return. His companions are recordings from his dead wife Helen, with garden designs she would never see. Then letters arrive from the opposite direction in time, through no channel Houston can explain. Sol Park, keeper of the unbuilt Sunward Station, writes: a leaking garden bay, a seam to weld twice, a garden he has not yet built. Bound by a promise Elias will one day exact, she refuses to say how or when he dies. Latency stretches each exchange across years. Director Amara Sy protects the correspondence while Elias redesigns the station around Sol's descriptions."},"the-night-orchestra":{"tagline":"Every tragedy was rehearsed first.","logline":"Disgraced violinist Anna Richter accepts a chair in the Aurelia Orchestra, an ensemble that performs only at 3 a.m. to empty halls — until she recognizes, in next week's program, the exact sequence of the concert that preceded her sister's death, note for note.","hook":"Vienna, 3 a.m.: a full orchestra plays at concert pitch to empty seats in a locked hall. For disgraced violinist Anna Richter — once a prodigy, now a wedding musician blacklisted after an on-stage breakdown — a second chair in the Aurelia is rescue, until the parts for next week's programme reach her stand: work for work, key for key, the concert she played the night her younger sister died. Then she turns the dedication page. It carries her own name.","shortSynopsis":"The Aurelia Orchestra has no audience, recordings or reviews: a locked Vienna hall, parts musicians may not take home, and 3 a.m. performances to empty seats, exactly as printed in Maestro Casimir Vane's catalogue. Newest chair Anna Richter, thirty-five, former prodigy whose solo career ended in breakdown, retains programmes note for note, including the concert played the night her younger sister died. At her third rehearsal, the programme is that concert. Her predecessor's margin notes warn: the orchestra does not predict tragedies; it performs them into rehearsal, and someone always paid for the season. Cellist Petr Novák confirms fragments; Inspector Greta Lindqvist found the same pattern in insurance data. Vane will not say if he is author, copyist, or prisoner."},"the-last-firekeeper":{"tagline":"Carry the light. Trust no dawn.","logline":"The sun is failing, and the Perpetual Hearth holds its seed: a flame that can be planted in the sky at the World's Edge. When the Hearth is betrayed from inside, scullery apprentice Ash, seventeen, takes the flame and runs — spending the world's last warmth village by village, while every kingdom decides whether she is a saviour, a thief, or a winter they cannot afford.","hook":"The sun is going out the way a hearth does — unevenly, with long cold mornings, crops greying at noon and seas skinning with ice in summer. In the scullery of the Perpetual Hearth, seventeen-year-old Ash tends other people's fires and is noticed by no one. Then the Hearth is betrayed from within, a clay lantern is thrust into her hands, and she runs — because the flame inside is the world's last living fire, and every village she lights from it spends what remains.","shortSynopsis":"For nine hundred years Firekeepers tended the Perpetual Hearth, flame from which cities relight forges and stoves. Now the sun fails, and the flame is its seed: living fire to be carried to the World's Edge and planted in the sky. The keeper who runs is Ash, seventeen, unlicensed apprentice. When the Hearth is betrayed from within, Master Corin gives her a clay lantern: run, and take no orders, including mine. Every fire lit from it burns it smaller. Ahead lie three kingdoms at war: a freezing forge-city asks fire itself; a frozen pass demands movement. Queen Sefira pursues her to buy one winter for her people. Captain Brandt must keep Ash alive, since the flame dies in untrained hands."},"the-memory-auction":{"tagline":"Your past is already sold.","logline":"When Vesper Exchange cataloguer June Park screens a routine lot and finds her own seventh birthday recorded through her mother's eyes, she discovers her entire childhood was sold in 41 lots — and the night her mother disappeared was never sold at all, but deposited in a sealed trust.","hook":"In 2041, the rich do not collect art. They collect other people's first kisses, summits and wedding mornings, legally, at the Vesper Exchange. June Park, 34, grades those lots for a living and believes her own childhood was wiped in an accident — until a routine screening plays her seventh birthday back through her mother's eyes. Her past was not lost. It was sold, in forty-one lots, by the guardian who raised her. One night was never listed at all.","shortSynopsis":"In 2041, at the Vesper Exchange, the wealthy legally buy other people's lived experiences, while sellers rarely can afford to buy. June Park, 34, a senior cataloguer, has built her life on not asking about her own childhood, supposedly lost in an accident and a therapeutic wipe. When lot 1147 shows her seventh birthday through her mother's eyes, she learns she was not wiped at once: she was sold, piece by piece, in forty-one lots. Buying it with money she does not have, she must bid, borrow, steal and trade up a buyer chain, guided by Elias Vane, while founder Odette Vesper guards one unlisted lot: the night her mother disappeared."},"blackwater-county":{"tagline":"Some towns bury more than their dead.","logline":"When a drought drops the reservoir in Blackwater County, Alabama, it exposes a car that went into the water in October 1989 — the month 17-year-old Ruth Ann Delaney vanished. Sheriff Dana Whitlock, who won her office by eleven votes, reopens a case her own predecessor helped bury, in a town that did not fail to solve the disappearance — it succeeded, together, at forgetting it.","hook":"When drought drops the Blackwater County reservoir, it gives back a car that went into the water in October 1989, the month seventeen-year-old Ruth Ann Delaney vanished. Sheriff Dana Whitlock won her office by eleven votes, promising competence, not crusades. The case file she inherits has been emptied by hand, her own predecessor signed a page out of it, and the family of the missing girl still owns the county bank. Blackwater did not fail to solve a disappearance. Together, it succeeded at forgetting one.","shortSynopsis":"Dana Whitlock, a former state investigator, returns to Blackwater County at forty-one and wins election as sheriff by eleven votes, the first outsider from beyond the families who have long supplied its sheriffs, bankers and commissioners. When drought lowers the reservoir, it exposes a car that entered the water in October 1989, the month seventeen-year-old Ruth Ann Delaney vanished after a church social. Dana finds Ruth Ann's file emptied by hand, the surviving removal page signed by her predecessor Hollis Brandt. Aided by Ruth Ann's brother Caleb, opposed quietly by Brandt and matriarch Pearl Delaney, she rebuilds the case interview by interview while the water keeps falling."},"seven-minutes-to-midnight":{"tagline":"The crash already happened. Answer anyway.","logline":"Ten years after Meridian Flight 217 crashed into the Atlantic and made her a widow, emergency dispatcher Mara Quinn takes a mayday on her console using the lost flight's call sign — in her husband's voice, describing tonight's weather, in contact windows exactly seven minutes long. She has until the fuel runs out, again, to work out whether she is being played, haunted, or given a second chance to land that plane.","hook":"At 11:53 p.m. on the tenth anniversary of Meridian Flight 217, dispatcher Mara Quinn answers a mayday on the lost flight's retired call sign. The voice is Captain Daniel Reyes, her husband, calm and precise, reporting the fuel emergency that preceded the Atlantic crash and asking her to keep him in the air. Contact lasts exactly seven minutes, then silence. Mara has one night, in rationed windows, to prove whether she is being played, haunted, or handed the truth.","shortSynopsis":"Mara Quinn, thirty-eight, has worked the graveyard shift at a regional emergency dispatch center for ten years, since Meridian Flight 217 crashed into the Atlantic and made her a widow. On the tenth anniversary, at 11:53 p.m., her console activates on a retired call sign. Captain Daniel Reyes reports a fuel emergency aboard Flight 217 and asks for Mara by name, in her husband's private phrasing. Each contact lasts exactly seven minutes. The caller knows unreleased cargo manifest details and describes tonight's weather, not the crash night's. Supervisory Agent Kate Lindholm seals the room, junior dispatcher Tommy Okafor finds earlier anniversary fragments, and together they anticipate one final, longer window."},"the-forgotten-astronaut":{"tagline":"Home is receiving. Home is wrong.","logline":"Stranded alone on a crippled station, astronaut Elias Ward finally re-establishes contact with Earth — an Earth where his mission never launched, where Miriam, his wife in his own life, has never met him, and where nobody remembers his name: a night-shift controller who has never heard it answers anyway, investigates, and finds a fragment of his voice in a corrupted archive.","hook":"Commander Elias Ward has kept himself sane for 214 days aboard the crippled station Meridian, alone since a debris strike killed his crew. When his repaired radio finally reaches Houston, Houston answers — courteously, competently, and with no record that he, his crew, or his mission ever existed. On this Earth, Miriam — his wife in the life he remembers — has never met him, and his daughter was never born. One night-shift trainee chooses to investigate anyway. His fight is not survival. It is being believed.","shortSynopsis":"For 214 days Commander Elias Ward, forty-seven, has run a station built for three alone, after a debris strike killed Pavel Orlov and Dr. Naomi Ibarra and wrecked communications. Each night he records messages for his daughter Sofia. When his rebuilt radio reaches Houston, the welcome is wrong: no Meridian mission appears in any register, the programme was cancelled in 2019, his wife Miriam has never met him, and Sofia does not exist. Trainee controller Ana Ruiz investigates off the books, testing him against her world's records, while Director Paul Hennig treats the transmissions as a probable foreign psychological operation and the station's oxygen, power and orbit deteriorate."},"the-city-without-shadows":{"tagline":"Darkness was outlawed. It went underground.","logline":"In Lumen, a city where the lights never go out and shadows are illegal, a crime-scene photographer develops a picture containing a shadow that should not exist — cast by a door that opens downward.","hook":"In Lumen, permanent artificial daylight ended an age of violence, and a shadow is evidence of illegal darkness. Vera Lindqvist photographs crime scenes for the Illumination Authority, trusting light to prove what happened. Then a photograph from a sealed, shadowless white room develops with a long shadow cast by a door hinged to open downward, a door in no plan of the building. To defend that one impossible image, Vera must ask what her city has hidden in the darkness it outlawed.","shortSynopsis":"Fifty years after the Blackout Riots, Lumen has abolished darkness: every occupied space is lit from several directions, nothing casts a shadow, and unregistered darkness is a crime. Vera Lindqvist, thirty-six, photographs crime scenes for the Illumination Authority and believes fully in the light. In a sealed white room, her developed photograph shows a long shadow falling from a door hinged to open downward, a door absent from the room and its plans. Reporting it brings suspicion, removal of the image and a reduced light ration at home. Following old plans downward, guided by Matteo Reyes, Vera finds the Umbral, a community in true darkness below, while Commissioner Hana Weiss defends the city above."},"the-last-summer-of-rome":{"tagline":"The empire ends. The road does not.","logline":"In 476 AD, an imperial courier rides the last dispatch of a dying empire across a collapsing Italy — carrying an order that could save Ravenna, start a war, or free him from Rome forever.","hook":"In the summer of 476, the Roman Empire in the West survives as paperwork, and Flavius Corvus carries it. A courier whose family served three generations without citizenship, he rides from Ravenna with the emperor's sealed order, while Placidia, his former lover, rides a parallel route with a contradictory one. One of the two orders is a forgery. Courted by the commander closing on the city and by a bishop building whatever comes next, Corvus must decide whether a courier is a man, or only an envelope.","shortSynopsis":"In the summer of 476 AD, the Western Empire survives from Ravenna as a court, a boy emperor and a delivery system of riders, relay stations and seals. Flavius Corvus, twenty-nine, half Roman, half Goth, has carried other men's orders for years without reading them. This summer his sealed dispatch is the court's last gesture toward rescue, making him the most courted man in Italy. Placidia, once his lover, rides a parallel route with a dispatch ordering the opposite course; one letter is a forgery. Ahead moves Odoacer, whose revolt has decided most of Italy's future and who offers Corvus the citizenship Rome withheld from his family for three generations."},"the-devils-frequency":{"tagline":"Don't answer after midnight","logline":"Overnight radio host Viv Cross despises her station's 'Ghost Week' stunt — until a caller named Ruthie describes the night she drowned in 1952 in details no living person could assemble, and the dead begin queueing to be heard, in the order of a single night in 1979.","hook":"On KDXL 1210 AM, overnight host Viv Cross screens every caller herself: seven seconds of delay, one dump button, and rules she trusts more than people. She despises Ghost Week, her station's ratings stunt, until a child named Ruthie calls live and describes, in verifiable period detail, the 1952 night she drowned. Viv checks, professionally. Every check confirms. Then the dead keep calling, in order, all week, and believing them on air becomes the most dangerous thing she does.","shortSynopsis":"KDXL, 1210 AM, broadcasts overnight from above a closed diner outside Bakersfield. Vivian Cross, forty-seven, once a network investigative reporter, hosts The Night Desk, screening her own calls and working a seven-second delay. During Ghost Week, a caller named Ruthie describes the 1952 flood in period detail. Ruthie Ellison drowned in 1952, aged nine. Viv checks names, dates and addresses; every check confirms. Then the dead keep calling, including a 1963 jazz pianist who asks whether the man who listens is still listening. While management sells sponsorships and debunker Denise Okafor-Pryce dismantles each call in public, Viv must decide, caller by caller, what a journalist owes a source who cannot consent or call back."},"the-great-escape-club":{"tagline":"Retirement was never the plan.","logline":"In 1968 six teenagers buried a bottle of whisky and a pact: whoever was left would drink it together at eighty. Now five survivors are residents of the same Leeds retirement home, the sixth is dying in its hospice wing, and management has refused the trip to Cornwall — so the Great Escape Club plans its breakout with military precision, at four miles an hour.","hook":"In 1968, six teenagers on a school trip to Cornwall buried a bottle of whisky and made a pact: whoever was left would drink it at eighty. Five of them are left, all residents of Sunnyvale Retirement Home in Leeds, and one of them, Arthur Bell, is dying in its hospice wing. Management has refused the journey south. So the five plan a breakout using the only skills retirement never took: a headmistress's timetable, a safe-cracker's memory, and a convoy that travels at four miles an hour.","shortSynopsis":"In 1968 six teenagers buried a bottle of whisky above a Cornish cove and promised whoever was left would dig it up and drink it at eighty. Fifty years later five survive, all living at Sunnyvale Retirement Home in Leeds. Arthur Bell is dying in its hospice wing and asks to be taken south to keep the promise. The home refuses on insurance, medication and consent grounds. So the Great Escape Club plans in the laundry room: former headmistress Mags Ellery, retired safe-cracker Stanley Kowalski, former getaway driver Frank Doyle, and Nell Brennan, who holds the current licence to drive a borrowed minibus, against deputy manager Priya Shah's competent watch."},"one-hundred-winters":{"tagline":"Some letters take a lifetime to arrive.","logline":"Separated by a border drawn through their valley in 1919, two lovers who can never meet again leave each other letters in the wall of a mountain lodge — a correspondence their grandchildren will finish a hundred years later.","hook":"In the winter of 1919, a new border is drawn through the Brenner valley, and through the engagement of Anna Wiesner, an Austrian schoolteacher, and Matteo Rinaldi, an Italian railway engineer. The mountain lodge where they met is suddenly in another country. Each will marry someone else. Neither will stop writing. Once a year, for sixty-one years, a letter waits in a loose stone of the lodge's hearth — and in 1985 Anna's granddaughter, restoring that lodge, finds them all.","shortSynopsis":"Before winter 1919, the Brenner valley is one world where German and Italian mix around a mountain lodge. Anna Wiesner, a northern-slope teacher, and Matteo Rinaldi, an engineer on the Brenner line, become engaged. The peace settlement then sets the Austria–Italy frontier along the Brenner watershed: the lodge in Italy, Anna's village in Austria. On the last night before winter closes the road, Matteo leaves the first letter behind a loose stone in the lodge hearth, beginning a schedule of one letter a year. Both later marry others, honour those marriages, and never meet in the room. In 1985 Anna's granddaughter Clara, restoring the lodge, finds sixty years of letters behind the stone."},"the-ocean-beneath-us":{"tagline":"We were not the first to go down.","logline":"Mapping an uncharted South Pacific trench four kilometres down, the research vessel Calypso Deep finds geometry where no geometry should be: worked stone channels, an ecosystem with no surface relatives, and lights in the canyon wall that answer its own in sequence. For marine geologist Dr. Ines Duarte, the discovery that could restore her career is also a place that has begun mapping her crew back — and her mining sponsor wants its coordinates.","hook":"Four kilometres beneath the South Pacific, marine geologist Dr. Ines Duarte leads the research vessel Calypso Deep through an uncharted trench — and its sonar returns geometry where no geometry should be: right angles at regular intervals, worked stone channels carrying warm water, and species with no surface relatives. Then lights in the canyon wall answer the submersible's own, in sequence. Duarte's mining sponsor wants the coordinates. Something below has begun mapping her crew back.","shortSynopsis":"The Calypso Deep is in the South Pacific on a seabed-mining consortium survey to map an uncharted trench system four kilometres down. Expedition lead Ines Duarte, thirty-nine, needs the voyage to be routine after her previous programme collapsed in a funding scandal that was not hers. Instead sonar repeatedly resolves right angles at regular intervals on the canyon floor. On the first dive, pilot Marcus Webb descends to stone channels carrying warm water and an ecosystem arranged around them, while biologist Dr. Yusuf Adeyemi catalogues species with no close surface relatives. Then lights in the canyon wall answer the submersible's lights in sequence. Aboard, consortium representative Claire Voss waits for the survey's coordinates, as a storm approaches."},"the-girl-who-stole-tomorrow":{"tagline":"Every today is paid for tomorrow.","logline":"Seventeen-year-old Wren Alvarez can borrow hours from her own future to fix today — but every borrowed day is deducted, with interest, from a life she hasn't lived yet, and her future self has started leaving warnings.","hook":"Seventeen-year-old Wren Alvarez of Albuquerque can borrow hours from her own future. The Alvarez women call the gift the Tithe, and its laws are carved inside her grandmother's kitchen cabinet: never borrow for yourself, never borrow twice in a moon, never check the balance. She saved her little brother with three borrowed hours and kept those laws for two years — until an audition and an evening she wanted too much broke them. Now warnings are arriving in her own handwriting, from the woman she is spending.","shortSynopsis":"Wren Alvarez, seventeen, lives with her grandmother in Albuquerque, where the women of her family carry the Tithe: pulling hours from their own future to spend in the present. Three laws carved in a kitchen cabinet govern it: never borrow for yourself, never twice in a moon, never check the balance. At fifteen, Wren pulled three hours to save her brother Nico after a car accident, and kept a secret ledger of hours owed. Now, tempted by a cello scholarship audition, she borrows again in small amounts. Notes arrive in her handwriting from herself at thirty-four, frightened and issuing colder instructions, while her best friend Dario Kim discovers he can borrow too."},"the-witness-list":{"tagline":"First they vanish the witnesses. Then the story.","logline":"Investigative reporter Sofia Marchetti has seven sources inside a defense-contract bribery ring. One by one they stop answering — not dead, not fled, but erased so thoroughly that even their families begin to doubt they existed.","hook":"Investigative reporter Sofia Marchetti has spent two years proving that a defense contractor bought a weapons program with bribes washed through veterans' charities, and her proof lives in seven confidential sources who answer only to her. Then one stops answering. He has not died or fled. His apartment is re-let, his desk reassigned, and his mother pours the tea while explaining that she has one son, not two. Nobody is being killed here. People are being un-happened, politely and legally, on paper.","shortSynopsis":"Sofia Marchetti of the Meridian Herald has spent two years building a case that a defense contractor bought a weapons program, moving money through veterans' charities so payments looked like gratitude. Her evidence lives in seven confidential sources, each holding one piece and known fully only to her, their identities in a paper notebook. When Source Three, a contracts auditor, misses his check-in, Sofia finds his apartment re-let, his desk reassigned, and his mother insisting she has one son, not two. Behind this stands the Veritas Reputation Group and director Helena Brandt, who correct records until memory files the correction. Sofia must publish before her sources, and her own record, are edited away."},"the-invisible-passenger":{"tagline":"He only exists when watched.","logline":"Night-train conductor Ruth Adler counts eleven passengers in coach four every night — twelve on the security feed. The twelfth figure always sits in seat 9, hands still, its face never resolving no matter how the footage is enhanced.","hook":"Night-train conductor Ruth Adler has run the Vienna–Venice train for nineteen years by three counts, tickets, heads, cameras, and they have always agreed. For six weeks they have not. Coach four holds eleven passengers when she walks it, twelve when she watches it: a figure in seat 9 whose face never resolves, whom nobody boards or sees standing, and no explanation survives. Ruth's evidence improves as her credibility fails. A psychological thriller about a woman who counts, and the one passenger she cannot account for.","shortSynopsis":"Ruth Adler, fifty-two, has conducted the overnight Vienna–Venice train for nineteen years, counting tickets, heads and cameras every night. Counting is personal: nineteen years ago her younger brother boarded a train on this route and never arrived, officially missing. For six weeks, coach four has broken her counts. She punches eleven tickets and counts eleven heads, but the security feed shows twelve, with a figure in seat 9 whose face never resolves and whom she never sees boarding. Security calls it a compression artifact; her supervisor suggests exhaustion. Ruth keeps an experiment log as the figure changes seats and leaves a folded paper dated 2004, the year her brother vanished."},"the-last-picture-house":{"tagline":"Some films were buried, not lost.","logline":"The last cinema in the county is three weeks from demolition when its owner finds, walled into the projection booth, the lost final reels of a 1950s director — including footage that rewrites who destroyed her.","hook":"The Rex, a 600-seat picture palace in a shrinking Ohio steel town, has three weeks before demolition. Its owner, Claire Fenn, 58, who signed the contract in exhaustion, is stripping the projection booth when she finds film cans walled inside it: the missing final reels of a 1956 film whose director was her great-aunt, erased from its credits. An archivist, a 91-year-old witness and a courteous studio buyer force one choice: sell the past, or project it once, in the room it was hidden for.","shortSynopsis":"Claire Fenn runs the Rex, a 600-seat picture palace in a shrinking Ohio steel town, alone as owner, projectionist, cleaner and bookkeeper. Exhausted and in debt, she has signed a demolition contract: in three weeks the Rex becomes a parking structure. In the projection booth she finds sealed film cans, holding the missing final reels of A Season in Hellfire, released in 1956 in a form that ended two careers. Star Vivian Marlowe was destroyed by scandal, and director Lydia Fenn, Claire's great-aunt, was struck from the credits. Archivist Elias Grant warns the fragile reels cannot simply be projected. Rebecca Sloane offers money if the reels pass to her company without a public screening."},"red-desert-rising":{"tagline":"The storm is coming. So is everyone else.","logline":"A meningitis outbreak gives Dr. Amara Haddad's mobile surgical convoy nine days and 400 kilometres of contested Sahel road. Pinned between two militias at the Wadi Khem crossing as a sixty-kilometre haboob bears down, the convoy carrying the region's only vaccine cold-chain has nowhere left to wait — and no road both sides will let it take.","hook":"In fictional Tazara, in the Sahel, Dr. Amara Haddad leads six trucks — a surgical theatre and the region's only vaccine cold-chain — toward a meningitis outbreak nine days, 400 kilometres away. At the Wadi Khem crossing, Rahal's northern militia pins her exposed convoy, an unseen southern column closes the track behind, ranging the crossing with shellfire, and a sixty-kilometre haboob bears down. Her patients cannot consent to these risks, her escort cannot win a fight it is forbidden to start, and her guide is a former smuggler the road's holders doubt.","shortSynopsis":"Dr. Amara Haddad leads a convoy that is both a mobile surgical unit of six trucks, carrying patients under her care, and the region's only vaccine cold-chain, which must stay within a safe temperature range across 400 kilometres of contested road. On her third tour, with nine days given, the convoy reaches the Wadi Khem crossing. Commander Ilyas Rahal's militia holds the only passable road and wants the cold-chain, while shelling from the south closes the track behind them. Sergeant Mariam Kone commands the escort, and guide Yusuf 'the Ledger' Bello, a former smuggler who owes both sides, stands between Amara and every negotiation. A haboob sixty kilometres wide moves toward the crossing."},"the-house-of-seven-doors":{"tagline":"Every family has seven histories.","logline":"Seven estranged siblings inherit Blackmoor House under one condition: spend a month deciding, together, which of them the house belongs to — in a home containing seven locked studies, each documenting a different family.","hook":"Seven estranged siblings return to Blackmoor House in Northumberland to bury their father and divide his fortune. Edmund Hale's will forbids both comforts: for thirty days they must live together in the house they all fled, opening the seven locked studies he prepared for them one by one, and at the month's end they must agree on a single heir, or lose everything to a foundation none of them has heard of. Each study contradicts the last. A gothic mystery about seven children, and seven incompatible fathers.","shortSynopsis":"Industrialist Edmund Hale is dead, and his seven children, born of three marriages and separated by decades of silence, are summoned to Blackmoor House in Northumberland for the reading of his will. They inherit jointly, may not sell the estate or leave it for thirty days, and must then name a single heir among themselves. If they cannot agree, everything passes to an unknown foundation, whose observer Julian Marsh watches the month. Blackmoor holds seven locked studies, one prepared by Edmund for each child, with keys held by housekeeper Agnes Crowe. Each archive tells a different family: in one Edmund is a saint, in another a thief who stole the company and the Hale name."},"the-perfect-alibi":{"tagline":"He nailed the role. So did the killer.","logline":"Washed-up TV detective Frankie Doyle finally gives the performance of his life — as a murderer, in an acting class, on the night of an identical real murder, in front of twelve witnesses who all agree he did it beautifully.","hook":"Frankie Doyle played Inspector Ray Cole for nine seasons and has played nothing since. Teaching acting in a Bristol community hall, he performs the confession scene he wrote — and that night a developer is murdered two streets away, exactly as Frankie performed it, including something that was never broadcast. Twelve students filmed him, so his alibi is perfect and his problem is total: the witnesses who prove he was elsewhere also prove he authored the crime. A crime comedy about a TV detective framed by his own fiction.","shortSynopsis":"Frankie Doyle, forty-nine, once played Inspector Ray Cole on Cole Justice for nine seasons and now teaches acting in a Bristol community hall. For the class showcase, filmed by twelve students on their phones, he performs his legendary scene: Cole confessing to a murder he did not commit to draw out the real killer. That night, two streets away, a property developer is found dead in circumstances following the episode, including a detail from a version audiences never saw. The footage gives Frankie a perfect alibi and a perfect problem: it proves he was in the hall, and that nobody else could describe the crime as he does. DI Sarah Okonkwo investigates."},"the-star-collector":{"tagline":"Put them back. All of them.","logline":"On the island of Tern, eleven-year-old Nia collects the stars that fall into the sea and trades their light to her neighbours — under her grandmother's one rule: a star may be kept for one night only, because the sky keeps accounts. The winter the lighthouse lamp cracks, Nia hoards forty stars to keep the island lit, and the sky begins, politely and then urgently, to empty.","hook":"On the island of Tern, eleven-year-old Nia, granddaughter of the lighthouse keeper, collects the stars that fall warm and humming into the sea, and trades their light to neighbours whose lamps have failed. Her grandmother Sefina allows one star, kept one night only, because the sky keeps accounts. Then the great lamp cracks, winter closes in, and Nia keeps forty stars to keep her island lit while the sky above it empties. An animated family adventure about love with bad arithmetic, and a collector coming politely down the dark to settle what is owed.","shortSynopsis":"On the island of Tern, fallen stars land in the sea as warm, humming, jar-sized lights that can light a room or guide a boat home. They are borrowed light: keeper Sefina's rule is that a star may be kept for one night only, then thrown back, because the sky keeps accounts. Her granddaughter Nia, eleven, is the island's collector, lifting stars from the shallows and keeping chalk marks for every star out and back. Her exception is Pip, the first star she caught. When the lighthouse lamp cracks in winter, Nia begins keeping stars to light the island. The jars multiply, the constellations fade, and the Lamplighter climbs down about an account."},"the-last-broadcast":{"tagline":"Stay on the air. Trust nothing you read.","logline":"When every network on Earth goes dark in the same hour, one regional TV station stays on air — and its producer discovers the emergency messages she is reading have been subtly, deliberately rewritten.","hook":"At 19:04 on a Tuesday, every network on Earth goes dark in the same hour — except Meridian 7, a failing regional station whose obsolete transmitter keeps it on air across three states. Producer Dana Reyes becomes the calm voice millions trust, reading the emergency bulletins exactly as they arrive. Then she discovers the bulletins are being rewritten before they reach her desk: small, deniable edits, in her own trusted voice. An apocalyptic thriller about the last channel on Earth, and the woman who must destroy her own authority to save her audience.","shortSynopsis":"At 19:04 on a Tuesday, satellite, cable and streaming die everywhere in the same hour, unexplained. One signal survives: Meridian 7, a struggling regional station whose decades-old transmitter suddenly carries three states, and whose nine staff become an emergency service. Producer Dana Reyes reads emergency bulletins exactly as written, delivered through the one working government line by a liaison calling himself Mr. Ash. On the third day she catches a bulletin changed between printer and desk, an evacuation order reversed, with nobody at the station responsible. Further edits follow, each small enough to be a typo and precise enough to endanger thousands. Engineer Eli Navarro finds proof of tampering in file metadata."},"the-republic-of-tomorrow":{"tagline":"Your future has already voted.","logline":"The Republic governs by the Foresight Index, which predicts crime before it happens — and election auditor Ingrid Halvorsen has certified it for a decade. Then she finds a sealed annex in her own file: a 91% prediction that next Tuesday she will commit a violent felony, alongside a stranger she will only meet because the state introduces them. Her fight is to prove a person is what they do, not what they were predicted to do.","hook":"Election auditor Ingrid Halvorsen has certified the Foresight Index for a decade: the predictive system by which the Republic forecasts crime, surges patrols and quietly consolidates polling places in districts it scores as dangerous. Then, auditing a disputed result, she opens a sealed annex in her own file. It predicts, with 91 percent confidence, that next Tuesday she will commit a violent felony, with a stranger the state will introduce her to for monitoring. A political thriller with no guns and no chase, about a woman audited by her own belief.","shortSynopsis":"Thirty years from now, the Republic governs by the Foresight Index, a model that surges patrols where violence is forecast and consolidates polling places in high-risk districts. Election auditor Ingrid Halvorsen, forty-five, has certified the system for a decade. Auditing a disputed result, she finds a sealed annex in her own file: the Index predicts with 91 percent confidence that she will commit a violent felony next Tuesday, at an address she has never visited, alongside Tomas Reyes, a school caretaker she has never met. The state introduces them for monitoring, narrows her clearance, notifies her insurer and moves her polling place. Ingrid appeals through a process designed to be survived rather than won."}};
